  "output": "## Context check first\n\nThis repo isn't a web/app codebase — it's the **JARVIS Obsidian vault**: markdown notes plus a handful of Node.js automation scripts (`70 Sistema/Automacao/`) and n8n workflow JSON. There's no database ORM, no React frontend, and no long-running server process. So two of your five categories don't have a literal target here:\n\n- **N+1 queries** → no DB. The closest analog is repeated filesystem I/O over the vault's markdown files.\n- **React re-renders** → no UI framework in this repo.\n\nI mapped the other three categories (caching, memory leaks, redundant computation) plus the filesystem/\"N+1\" analog onto the actual code: `vault-lint/lint.mjs`, `morning-brief/lib/vault.mjs`, and `morning-brief/lib/brief.mjs`. Findings below are real, with file:line references.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Redundant full-vault re-walks (\"N+1\"-equivalent filesystem I/O)\n\n`70 Sistema/Automacao/vault-lint/lint.mjs:271-283` walks the entire vault tree **four separate times**:\n\n```js\nwalkMd(vaultRoot, vaultRoot, notes);                              // full walk #1\nwalkMd(path.join(vaultRoot, \"90 Arquivo\"), vaultRoot, extras, ...);      // walk #2\nwalkMd(path.join(vaultRoot, \"70 Sistema\", \"Templates\"), vaultRoot, extras, ...); // walk #3\nwalkMd(vaultRoot, vaultRoot, extras, [\".git\",\"node_modules\",\".obsidian\",\".claude\"], [\".canvas\",\".m4a\",\".png\",\".jpg\",\".pdf\",\".base\"]); // full walk #4 — re-walks EVERYTHING again\n```\n\nWalk #4 re-traverses the same directory tree as walk #1, re-reading every directory entry, just to pick up non-`.md` attachments. As the vault grows this multiplies directory reads/`readFileSync` calls needlessly.\n\n**Fix:** collect everything in one pass — gather `.md` notes and non-`.md` targets in the same `readdirSync` loop:\n\n```js\nfunction walkAll(dir, vaultRoot, notesOut, extrasOut, skip) {\n  let entries;\n  try { entries = fs.readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true }); } catch { return; }\n  for (const ent of entries) {\n    const full = path.join(dir, ent.name);\n    const rel = path.relative(vaultRoot, full).replace(/\\\\/g, \"/\");\n    if (ent.isDirectory()) {\n      if (skip.some((s) => rel === s || rel.startsWith(`${s}/`))) continue;\n      walkAll(full, vaultRoot, notesOut, extrasOut, skip);\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (ent.name.endsWith(\".md\")) {\n      notesOut.push(readNote(full, rel, ent.name));\n    } else if (/\\.(canvas|m4a|png|jpg|pdf|base)$/.test(ent.name)) {\n      extrasOut.push({ rel, name: ent.name, basename: ent.name.replace(/\\.[^.]+$/, \"\") });\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Synchronous per-link `fs.existsSync` in a loop (real N+1 pattern)\n\n`vault-lint/lint.mjs:220-222`, inside `resolve()`, called once per wikilink match across every note:\n\n```js\ntry {\n  if (fs.existsSync(path.join(vaultRoot, alvo.replace(/\\\\$/, \"\")))) return true;\n} catch { /* ... */ }\n```\n\nFor a vault with hundreds of notes and multiple `[[links]]` each, this is one blocking disk stat *per link*, instead of reusing data you already have. `walkMd` already knows every directory it visited (`ent.isDirectory()`) — build a `Set` of folder paths during the walk once, and check membership in-memory:\n\n```js\n// during walkMd, when ent.isDirectory():\nfolderSet.add(rel.toLowerCase());\n// resolve() becomes:\nconst resolve = (alvo) => {\n  const key = alvo.toLowerCase().replace(/\\\\$/, \"\").replace(/\\.md$/, \"\");\n  return alvos.has(key) || alvos.has(key.split(\"/\").pop()) || folderSet.has(key);\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Duplicated parsing logic = duplicated work + drift risk\n\n`parseFrontmatter` / `parseSimpleYaml` / `unquote` exist **twice**, nearly byte-for-byte identical, in `vault-lint/lint.mjs:101-138` and `morning-brief/lib/vault.mjs:60-113`. Every scheduled run (dashboard, morning-brief, vault-lint) independently re-implements and re-executes the same frontmatter parse over the same files.\n\n**Fix:** extract a shared `70 Sistema/Automacao/lib/frontmatter.mjs` and import it from both. This is the \"caching opportunity\" that matters most here — not runtime memoization, but eliminating triplicate full-vault parsing across three separately-scheduled scripts. If all three ran back-to-back (they're all Task-Scheduler jobs on the same vault), a shared on-disk snapshot (`.cache/vault-index.json`, invalidated by directory mtime) would let `dashboard.mjs` and `lint.mjs` skip a second full parse if `morning-brief` just ran.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Redundant computation: overlapping filters/sorts over the same task list\n\n`morning-brief/lib/brief.mjs` — `selectTop3` (line 33), `collectRisks` (line 87), and `collectToday` (line 177) each independently `.filter()` + `.sort(compareTasks)` the **same** `tasks` array with overlapping \"not done + has due date\" predicates:\n\n```js\n// selectTop3\nconst eligible = tasks.filter((t) => !t.done && t.due && toDate(t.due) < tomorrow && !isRecurringStale(t, today));\n// collectRisks\nconst overdueTasks = tasks.filter((t) => !t.done && t.due && toDate(t.due) < toDate(today) && ...).sort(compareTasks).slice(0, 5);\n// collectToday\nconst todayTasks = tasks.filter((t) => !t.done && ((t.due && t.due === today) || ...)).sort(compareTasks);\n```\n\nAt current vault scale this is cheap, but it's three separate O(n log n) passes for what could be one filter pass with pre-bucketed results (`todayTasks`, `overdueTasks`, `futureTasks`) reused across all three brief sections. Also `toDate(today)` gets re-parsed from string on every loop iteration in `collectRisks`/`collectCommercial` instead of once outside the loop — trivial but free to fix:\n\n```js\nconst tDate = toDate(today); // compute once, pass down instead of calling toDate(today) per iteration\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Memory leaks\n\nNot applicable in the classic sense — every script here (`dashboard.mjs`, `generate.mjs`, `lint.mjs`) is a one-shot CLI process invoked by Task Scheduler that exits (`process.exit(...)`), so there's no long-lived heap to leak. The one place with genuine long-running state is the **self-hosted n8n instance** (`70 Sistema/Automacao/n8n-selfhost/`) — if you want, I can look at whether its execution-log retention/pruning is configured (unbounded execution history is n8n's most common \"leak\" complaint), but that's a config check, not a code fix, and I haven't verified it yet — say the word if you want me to check `start-local.ps1` / the n8n env config for `EXECUTIONS_DATA_PRUNE`.\n\n---\n\n**Priority if you want me to implement:** #1 and #2 (single-pass walk + in-memory folder set) are the highest-value, lowest-risk fixes — they cut vault-lint's I/O by roughly 4x with no behavior change. Want me to make those edits?\n",
